ponent\'s pieces, which is a good move.\n\nI verify my board math to be sure. I check for valid moves, that I\'ve accounted for all possibilities and it checks out.\n\nMy final choice is (F, 8). It seems the most strategically sound move with the current board state.\n\n\n', 'output': "Situation: The game is in the mid-to-late phase. I hold the top-right corner (A8) and some edge pieces, while the opponent has a solid block in the center-left. The corners A1, H1, and H8 are still empty. It is crucial to grab stable edge positions without giving up corners.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(B, 1)**: This is a C-square move adjacent to the empty corner A1. Analysis shows that flipping B2 and B3 would allow the opponent to utilize the diagonal (C3-B2-A1) to capture the corner A1. This is a blunder.\n- **(G, 8)**: Another C-square move adjacent to H8. It risks giving the opponent the corner H8, especially since the diagonal control is contested.\n- **(D, 2), (C, 2), (E, 2)**: These are reasonable internal moves that flip pieces and maintain centralization, but they don't secure structural territory like an edge.\n- **(F, 8)**: This is a strong edge move. It flips opponent pieces at F7, F6, and G7.\n    - It uses my piece at H6 to flip G7.\n    - It uses my piece at F5 to flip F7 and F6.\n    - By occupying F8, I extend my control on the right edge (Column 8) where I already have A8.\n    - Crucially, it does not give the opponent immediate access to the H8 corner because the diagonal C3-F6 is blocked (F6 flips to @), and there are no valid sandwiching moves for the opponent to take H8 immediately. It also limits the opponent's valid moves in that quadrant.\n\nReason: (F, 8) is the best strategic choice. It secures a valuable edge position, gains material by flipping three pieces, and is tactically safe as it does not surrender the adjacent corner. It restricts the opponent's mobility on the right side of the board.\n\nAnswer: (F, 8)"}
