nternal discs. While gaining a corner is good, flipping many internal pieces ("noisy move") often creates new move opportunities for the opponent by providing them with new anchors.\n2. Move (H, 8) flips only one disc (G8). This is a "quiet move" that increases my score and stability without giving the opponent much material to work with. Furthermore, flipping G8 secures the right edge (Column 8) since I already hold F8 and A8. This creates a solid block of stable pieces.\n\nReason: H8 is the superior move because it secures a corner and consolidates the right edge while flipping minimal pieces, thereby restricting the opponent\'s future mobility. A1 remains safe from the opponent for now (as they lack the necessary anchors to take it), so it can likely be taken on a subsequent turn.\n\nAnswer: (H, 8)'}
