ily on the edges and in the center. The key is to consolidate territory without giving the opponent access to the remaining corner (H1) or valid edge moves.\n\nEvaluation:\nI evaluated a few promising moves:\n1.  **(H, 5)**: This is an edge move. It places a piece at H5, which sandwiches the opponent\'s vertical line of pieces (G5, F5, E5) between H5 and my existing piece at D5 (D5 is \'@\'). This flips 3 pieces. H5 is supported by my pieces at H6 and H8. It is currently safe from being flipped back as the opponent cannot play at H4 or H7 to sandwich it. It secures the bottom-right edge.\n2.  **(D, 7)**: This move flips D6 and E7. It fills a gap in the upper-right quadrant, creating a solid block of pieces connected to my corner at A8/H8. It is a very stable move but plays internally rather than taking the edge.\n3.  **(G, 3)**: A high-impact move flipping 8 pieces. However, it places a piece at G3, adjacent to the H2/H1 danger zone, potentially giving the opponent tactical chances near the empty H1 corner.\n4.  **(C, 2)**: Flips pieces diagonally but opens up the C1 edge square to the opponent, which is adjacent to my A1 corner. While A1 is safe, allowing play near corners is unnecessary.\n\nReason:\n**(H, 5)** is the best choice because it is a "quiet" and stable edge move. By playing H5, I connect my existing edge pieces (H6, H8) and solidify control over the right-hand side of the board. It flips a vertical line of opponent pieces in column 5, turning them into a wall of my color. Crucially, it does not give the opponent any immediate counter-play on the H-edge (H4 is invalid for them), whereas internal moves might leave more openings. Securing stable edge pieces is a priority to close out the game.\n\nAnswer: (H, 5)'}
