e (D, 8) is available on the edge.\n\nEvaluation: The move (D, 8) places a piece on the right edge. It flips the opponent\'s piece at C7 (diagonal) and the pieces at E7 and F6 (diagonal).\nCrucially, by flipping E7 from \'*\' to \'@\', this move removes the opponent\'s anchor required to play at E8 (the empty square below D8). Since E8 is an edge square, the opponent would typically need a piece at E7 (or D7/F7 diagonals) to access it. With E7 flipped to my color, and D7/F7 already controlled or empty in a way that doesn\'t help the opponent, the opponent cannot play E8. This allows me to play E8 on my next turn, effectively securing the entire bottom-right edge block (D8-H8).\nAdditionally, flipping C7 hinders the opponent\'s ability to play at B8, further restricting their mobility.\n\nReason: (D, 8) is the best move because it acts as a "quiet" edge move that sets up a guaranteed follow-up at E8. It stabilizes the right edge, connects to my existing strongholds, and reduces the opponent\'s valid move options by removing their anchors for key edge squares.\n\nAnswer: (D, 8)'}
