iece at D8. Since A8 is already mine, playing B8 creates a continuous stable chain of pieces (A8-B8-C8-D8) along the edge.\n2. **(H, 4)**: This moves onto the bottom edge. While valid, it sits in an unconnected position near the empty corner H1 and the opponent's piece at H3, which could offer the opponent tactical chances to destabilize the edge or aim for the corner later.\n3. **(G, 2)**: This is an X-square move adjacent to the empty corner H1. Playing here without a guaranteed sequence to take the corner is generally a blunder, as it often surrenders the corner to the opponent.\n\nReason: The move (B, 8) is strategically superior because it consolidates the right edge. By connecting the new piece to the existing corner (A8) and flipping C8, I create a permanent wall of discs that the opponent cannot flip back. This maximizes stability and simplifies the game in my favor.\n\nAnswer: (B, 8)"}
