e late stages with 19 empty spaces remaining. I control the H8 corner. The H-row presents a significant opportunity: the segment H3-H7 consists entirely of opponent pieces ('*'), sandwiched between my piece at H8 and the empty square at H2.\n\nEvaluation: The valid move (H, 2) is exceptionally strong. It flips five opponent pieces (H3 through H7) and establishes a connected chain of my pieces from H2 to H8. Since I already own the H8 corner, this entire edge becomes stable territory that the opponent cannot flip back.\nI also considered the risk of the opponent taking the adjacent corner H1. However, the opponent currently lacks a valid move to G2 or any other square that would serve as an anchor to capture H1. Even if they could eventually take H1, it would not flip my established H-edge because H8 acts as a secure anchor.\n\nReason: Taking the edge with (H, 2) secures a massive swing of 5 pieces and locks down the bottom edge of the board by connecting to my existing corner at H8. This move maximizes stable disc count and leaves the opponent with very limited options in that sector, without any immediate risk of a counter-attack that could reclaim those pieces.\n\nAnswer: (H, 2)"}
